1
00:00:00,180 --> 00:00:01,720
You've probably seen the show,

2
00:00:02,180 --> 00:00:03,800
the control room, the panic,

3
00:00:04,180 --> 00:00:05,360
the man they made, the villain.

4
00:00:06,060 --> 00:00:08,880
Almost everyone has an image of Chernobyl in their head by now.

5
00:00:10,060 --> 00:00:14,420
But the actual Soviet government spent six years quietly changing its own story about

6
00:00:14,420 --> 00:00:17,600
what really caused it. In 1986,

7
00:00:18,440 --> 00:00:20,560
the world was told it was the operator's fault.

8
00:00:21,200 --> 00:00:25,660
In 1992, a second, quieter report reversed that.

9
00:00:25,660 --> 00:00:30,580
This time, blame fell on the reactor itself and a floor the men in that control room

10
00:00:30,580 --> 00:00:32,000
were never even told about.

11
00:00:34,180 --> 00:00:35,460
That second report is real,

12
00:00:35,880 --> 00:00:38,180
it is public, and it tells a story that is,

13
00:00:38,520 --> 00:00:41,500
in places, quietly different from the one you watched.

14
00:00:42,860 --> 00:00:44,920
This is not another retelling of Chernobyl.

15
00:00:45,240 --> 00:00:46,480
This is the official report,

16
00:00:46,800 --> 00:00:50,960
the actual physics, and exactly where the story you know parts ways with it.

17
00:00:52,140 --> 00:00:53,720
You're watching Black Box Breakdown.

18
00:00:55,080 --> 00:00:58,260
Chernobyl, the official report versus the show.

19
00:00:59,420 --> 00:01:02,520
Chapter one, the floor nobody was told about.

20
00:01:03,820 --> 00:01:07,520
Reactor number four at the Chernobyl plant in Soviet Ukraine had been running for barely

21
00:01:07,520 --> 00:01:10,900
two years. It used a design called the RBMK,

22
00:01:11,240 --> 00:01:12,740
built with graphite and water,

23
00:01:13,160 --> 00:01:16,240
cheaper and easier to mass produce than the reactors the West was building.

24
00:01:17,660 --> 00:01:20,380
It also, unlike almost every Western reactor,

25
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:22,880
had no full containment building around it.

26
00:01:23,300 --> 00:01:25,680
If something inside ever truly went wrong,

27
00:01:26,120 --> 00:01:27,860
there was nothing built to hold it in.

28
00:01:29,080 --> 00:01:31,000
And it had a floor baked into its physics.

29
00:01:31,500 --> 00:01:34,740
In a normal reactor, if the cooling water starts to boil away,

30
00:01:35,120 --> 00:01:37,340
the reaction slows down. In this one,

31
00:01:37,720 --> 00:01:41,520
the opposite happened. Losing water made the reaction speed up.

32
00:01:43,650 --> 00:01:45,790
Engineers call it a positive void coefficient.

33
00:01:46,430 --> 00:01:48,430
In plain terms, at low power,

34
00:01:48,770 --> 00:01:50,790
this reactor did not want to calm down.

35
00:01:51,150 --> 00:01:54,810
It wanted to run away. There was a second floor,

36
00:01:55,090 --> 00:01:58,570
just as dangerous. The emergency shutdown rods had graphite tips.

37
00:01:58,970 --> 00:02:02,090
For a split second, pushing them in didn't slow the reaction down.

38
00:02:02,650 --> 00:02:06,850
It sped it up first. This was not a total secret.

39
00:02:07,370 --> 00:02:09,890
11 years earlier, at a sister plant in Leningrad,

40
00:02:10,110 --> 00:02:15,430
an almost identical reactor suffered a partial meltdown rooted in the exact same physics.

41
00:02:15,950 --> 00:02:17,290
It was never discussed openly.

42
00:02:17,670 --> 00:02:19,410
The fix waited until after Chernobyl.

43
00:02:20,370 --> 00:02:24,410
The men running Reactor 4 on the night of April 26th,

44
00:02:24,450 --> 00:02:31,530
1986, knew none of this. That

45
00:02:31,530 --> 00:02:33,810
night, the crew was running a safety test.

46
00:02:34,210 --> 00:02:37,910
Could the turbine's own leftover spin generate enough power to run the cooling pumps

47
00:02:37,910 --> 00:02:41,670
during a blackout, just for the 40 seconds until backup generators kicked in?

48
00:02:42,910 --> 00:02:44,550
The test should have run that afternoon.

49
00:02:45,390 --> 00:02:47,490
Kiev's grid operator asked for a delay,

50
00:02:47,890 --> 00:02:50,650
needing the power. So it slipped to the middle of the night,

51
00:02:50,850 --> 00:02:54,510
onto a different, less experienced crew than the one who'd planned it.

52
00:02:55,790 --> 00:02:58,170
To hold the Reactor at the right power for the test,

53
00:02:58,670 --> 00:03:00,430
most of the control rods were pulled out,

54
00:03:00,890 --> 00:03:02,610
far more than safety rules allowed.

55
00:03:03,090 --> 00:03:05,410
That single decision would matter enormously.

56
00:03:07,270 --> 00:03:09,730
At 1 .23 in 40 seconds in the morning,

57
00:03:10,050 --> 00:03:11,650
with the reactor already unstable,

58
00:03:12,250 --> 00:03:15,870
shift supervisor Alexander Akimov gave the order to shut it all down.

59
00:03:17,770 --> 00:03:19,150
Because of that graphite tip floor,

60
00:03:19,490 --> 00:03:21,090
in a reactor already unbalanced,

61
00:03:21,430 --> 00:03:23,890
hitting the emergency shutdown did not calm it down.

62
00:03:24,510 --> 00:03:28,450
It spiked power instead. Fuel channels ruptured.

63
00:03:28,850 --> 00:03:31,090
Two explosions followed, seconds apart.

64
00:03:31,650 --> 00:03:36,290
The second blew the 1 ,000 -ton roof off the reactor building and set the exposed graphite

65
00:03:36,290 --> 00:03:37,930
core on fire in the open air.

66
00:03:39,110 --> 00:03:41,790
Alexander Akimov and the young engineer at the controls,

67
00:03:42,350 --> 00:03:45,110
Leonid Toptunov, both stayed at the plant afterward,

68
00:03:45,750 --> 00:03:47,410
still believing the reactor was intact.

69
00:03:47,870 --> 00:03:50,470
Both died of radiation sickness within three weeks.

70
00:03:51,990 --> 00:03:54,230
Chapter three, the story that changed.

71
00:03:55,830 --> 00:03:57,610
Months later, at a meeting in Vienna,

72
00:03:57,950 --> 00:04:00,930
the Soviet Union gave the world its first official explanation.

73
00:04:01,450 --> 00:04:03,750
It was led by a chemist named Valerie Legasov.

74
00:04:04,810 --> 00:04:07,050
That first report blamed the operators.

75
00:04:07,630 --> 00:04:09,830
Rule violations, a bad test,

76
00:04:10,110 --> 00:04:13,710
human error. The reactor's own design was mentioned only briefly,

77
00:04:14,150 --> 00:04:17,470
almost as an aside. Six years later,

78
00:04:17,730 --> 00:04:21,370
a second, far less publicized report reversed that finding.

79
00:04:23,030 --> 00:04:27,690
This is that report, published by the International Atomic Energy Agency in 1992.

80
00:04:28,310 --> 00:04:30,510
Its own words, according to the first report,

81
00:04:30,810 --> 00:04:32,470
the main cause was the operators.

82
00:04:33,110 --> 00:04:36,530
According to this one, the main cause was the reactor's design.

83
00:04:37,890 --> 00:04:39,130
The men in that control room,

84
00:04:39,310 --> 00:04:44,690
it turns out, had been set up to fail by a machine with two known hidden floors and

85
00:04:44,690 --> 00:04:45,890
then blamed for the result.

86
00:04:48,150 --> 00:04:50,890
Alitaly Dyatlov, the engineer supervising that night,

87
00:04:51,130 --> 00:04:54,210
was convicted in 1987 and served three years.

88
00:04:54,650 --> 00:04:58,790
He spent the rest of his life arguing he'd been blamed for a flaw nobody told him existed.

89
00:04:59,390 --> 00:05:02,990
The 1992 report says he had a point without clearing him entirely.

90
00:05:04,130 --> 00:05:06,410
And the cover -up ran deeper than the trial.

91
00:05:06,930 --> 00:05:11,010
Pripyat's 49 ,000 residents weren't evacuated for over 36 hours.

92
00:05:11,550 --> 00:05:13,770
The May Day parade in Kiev went ahead anyway.

93
00:05:14,330 --> 00:05:18,150
The world only found out because radiation alarms went off at a power plant in Sweden

94
00:05:18,150 --> 00:05:22,010
hundreds of miles away. Valerie Legasov,

95
00:05:22,430 --> 00:05:23,650
the man who delivered that first,

96
00:05:23,930 --> 00:05:28,150
incomplete report, secretly recorded his own account on cassette tapes before he died.

97
00:05:28,730 --> 00:05:31,210
He was found dead in his apartment on April 27,

98
00:05:31,770 --> 00:05:34,950
1988, two years and one day after the disaster.

99
00:05:36,610 --> 00:05:38,630
Chapter four, the show versus the report.

100
00:05:40,090 --> 00:05:45,210
HBO's Chernobyl in 2019 got the physics right and the men who

101
00:05:45,210 --> 00:05:48,750
cleared radioactive graphite off that rooftop by hand were real,

102
00:05:49,290 --> 00:05:51,050
exactly as brutal as depicted.

103
00:05:52,470 --> 00:05:55,630
But the show never puts that 1992 reversal on screen.

104
00:05:56,070 --> 00:05:58,010
It ends on Legasov's private crusade,

105
00:05:58,430 --> 00:06:00,930
closer to the 1986 story than the corrected one.

106
00:06:02,390 --> 00:06:04,230
One of its central scientists,

107
00:06:04,750 --> 00:06:06,450
Ulana Komyuk, never existed.

108
00:06:06,890 --> 00:06:09,410
The show says so itself in its own credits.

109
00:06:10,130 --> 00:06:12,150
She stands in for several real people,

110
00:06:12,410 --> 00:06:13,910
folded into one for clarity.

111
00:06:15,270 --> 00:06:19,850
The three divers sent to drain water beneath the reactor in the show are framed as a

112
00:06:19,850 --> 00:06:21,910
suicide mission. In reality,

113
00:06:22,350 --> 00:06:27,310
all three survived. One of them has publicly said nobody threw them a hero's welcome

114
00:06:27,310 --> 00:06:30,390
afterward because nothing about it felt heroic at the time.

115
00:06:31,410 --> 00:06:35,450
And you may have heard the story of a bridge where towns people gathered to watch the

116
00:06:35,450 --> 00:06:37,970
fire glow and all later died from the radiation.

117
00:06:38,630 --> 00:06:41,030
That story has no real death record behind it.

118
00:06:41,430 --> 00:06:44,110
Most serious historians treat it as an urban legend,

119
00:06:44,310 --> 00:06:46,310
one the show itself may have helped spread.

120
00:06:48,310 --> 00:06:49,750
None of this makes the show bad.

121
00:06:50,370 --> 00:06:53,230
Its own writer has said the goal was never to sensationalize,

122
00:06:53,570 --> 00:06:58,090
only to compress a true, complicated story into something people would actually watch.

123
00:06:58,650 --> 00:07:01,270
That's just not always the same thing as the official record.

124
00:07:02,630 --> 00:07:04,550
Chapter five, what it actually cost.

125
00:07:06,390 --> 00:07:08,230
31 people died in the first weeks,

126
00:07:08,490 --> 00:07:10,310
officially. Two instantly,

127
00:07:10,670 --> 00:07:12,690
28 from radiation sickness,

128
00:07:13,130 --> 00:07:18,550
one from a heart attack. The long -term toll is where honest disagreement starts.

129
00:07:18,990 --> 00:07:23,370
The World Health Organization estimates up to around 4 ,000 eventual cancer deaths among

130
00:07:23,370 --> 00:07:27,410
the most exposed. Other independent estimates run past 90 ,000.

131
00:07:27,410 --> 00:07:32,130
Nobody has settled this. And anyone who tells you they have is picking a number to suit

132
00:07:32,130 --> 00:07:35,310
their argument. What is not disputed?

133
00:07:35,830 --> 00:07:39,770
Somewhere between 600 ,000 and 800 ,000 people were mobilized to contain it.

134
00:07:40,170 --> 00:07:42,030
Some of them, nicknamed biorobots,

135
00:07:42,310 --> 00:07:47,070
cleared radioactive debris off the roof by hand in shifts as short as tens of seconds

136
00:07:47,070 --> 00:07:50,170
because the actual robot's electronics failed in the radiation.

137
00:07:51,630 --> 00:07:56,330
The original hastily built shelter was replaced in 2016 by a new steel arch,

138
00:07:56,330 --> 00:07:58,590
slid into place over five days,

139
00:07:58,870 --> 00:08:02,550
funded by dozens of countries built to last 100 years.

140
00:08:04,150 --> 00:08:06,350
Pripyat, the city built for the plant's workers,

141
00:08:06,610 --> 00:08:09,070
is still empty 40 years later.

142
00:08:09,690 --> 00:08:11,230
A handful of elderly residents,

143
00:08:11,650 --> 00:08:13,710
mostly women in their 70s and 80s,

144
00:08:13,890 --> 00:08:17,050
quietly moved back in anyway and were never forced back out.

145
00:08:18,510 --> 00:08:20,470
What stays with me here isn't the explosion.

146
00:08:21,150 --> 00:08:24,210
It's the six years it took for the truth to catch up to the story.

147
00:08:25,270 --> 00:08:28,030
The men in that control room were blamed first,

148
00:08:28,330 --> 00:08:29,990
and only partly unblamed,

149
00:08:30,250 --> 00:08:31,650
long after it mattered to them.

150
00:08:33,030 --> 00:08:35,770
Great television compresses a true story to make it watchable.

151
00:08:36,110 --> 00:08:38,710
It's still worth knowing exactly where the compression happened.

152
00:08:40,680 --> 00:08:43,960
If a report that quietly corrected itself six years later got you,

153
00:08:44,300 --> 00:08:48,120
wait until you see the actual black box report behind the Boeing 737 MAX.

154
